# Chapter 5, part 4: the markers from LaTeX to Printer, without those that are
# printed as a label (they are in markers5).

.h2 LaTeX [marker-latex]
.ix LaTeX marker
.ix Marker, LaTeX
.ix Instructions to LaTeX
.ix Typesetting instructions
.ix Raw LaTeX
A LaTeX marker holds instructions for LaTeX, the typesetting system that makes
the pages of a print, and the Printer hands them to LaTeX exactly as you typed
them.
Use it for anything that LaTeX can do and that Strata has no marker for: a
rule of a special weight, a table with its own borders, a mathematical
display, a change of the space between lines.  The marker stands in the text
where its result is wanted, in a line of text or in a block of its own.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | The text editor only (the status line shows LATEX EDIT); there are no
 other fields.
Print | Typeset: the text of the marker, as it is typed.
.end
.h3 How it is entered
{k:Alt-F12}, LaTeX opens a full editor for the instructions.  Type them as
LaTeX requires them.  {k:Ins} saves them and inserts the marker, {k:Esc} throws
them away.  To change the text, put the cursor on the marker and press
{k:Alt-F12}: the editor opens with the text.  The text is kept as it is typed:
the ends of lines, the tabs and the spaces are not changed, and a line is never
wrapped for the printer.  A character that is not on the keyboard, such as an
accented letter, is printed as that letter; a nonbreaking space typed with
{k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} stays a nonbreaking space.
.pic pictures/markers-latex-edit.png 5 The editor of a LaTeX marker, with the text of a centered rule.
.h3 On the screen
The marker shows as its label, LaTeX instructions, between square brackets
and in the color of markers (its Short form), unless it is shown as a picture
({n:marker-latex-picture}).  In the X11 version the word LaTeX is drawn as
LaTeX draws its own name: the A is a smaller letter that is raised, and the E
is lowered.  The terminal version shows the plain letters.  The other display
modes show a diamond (Diamond), the word LaTeX (Name) or the words LaTeX
instructions (Short and Long).  The text of the marker is not shown in the
document; the editor of the marker shows it.  A text export and the picture of
the screen have the plain words.
.pic pictures/markers-latex-label.png 5.1 LaTeX markers on the screen of the X11 version: the logo and the bold word are pictures; the centered rule is a block, so it is a label.
.h3 As a picture on the screen [marker-latex-picture]
.ix LaTeX marker, as a picture
.ix Picture, of a LaTeX marker
.ix Inline, display mode of the LaTeX marker
The display mode of the LaTeX marker is Inline, like that of the Equation, the
Picture and the Keycap, and Inline is the default mode of the type.  In the
X11 version a LaTeX marker whose mode is Inline is shown on the screen as what
it prints, in the place where the marker stands, as an equation is: the logo
{c:\TeX{{}}}, a word in bold, a symbol, a formula, a rule of a set length, a box
or the large logo of the title page.  A picture that is as tall as a line of text, or
a little taller, stands on the baseline of the line, and the line is as tall as
the picture needs.  The picture has the size that the same instructions have
in the print, next to the other words of the line.  It is made while the
author works: until it is ready the marker shows as its label, no key waits for
it, and the picture replaces the label when it is ready.  A picture that was
made once is kept, so a document that is opened again shows its pictures at
once.
A picture that is taller than four rows of the line is a block.  It takes rows
of its own, centered: the text before the marker ends its line, the picture
follows on as many rows as it needs, and the text after the marker goes on
below it, as it does for a Picture marker that is tall.  The height of a
picture is not limited.  Its size is limited as that of a Picture marker is, to
4000 by 4000 pixels, and a picture that is larger is shown in the Short form.
A block makes the print and the screen as tall as it is, and the author deals
with that.
The cursor treats the picture as one thing, as it does an equation or a
picture: one key moves onto it and one key off it, and a frame surrounds it when
the cursor is on it.  {k:Alt-F12} on it opens the editor of the instructions,
and the picture is made again for the new text.
{b:The rule.}  The Editor gives the instructions of the marker to LaTeX alone,
and the result determines it.  No list of instructions that are refused is kept
beforehand.
- If LaTeX reports an error, the marker is shown in its Short form.
- If LaTeX takes more than five seconds, the marker is shown in its Short form.
- If the result has nothing to see, the marker is shown in its Short form.  A
  marker that only sets something that goes on after it (a font size, a color,
  a definition, a package, a counter), breaks a page, or makes only space gives
  such a result.
- In every other case the result is shown as a picture.
A marker that is shown in the Short form is shown so also when LaTeX is not
installed, and when the picture is larger than the limit.  The same rule holds
for every marker that can be shown Inline (Equation, Picture, Keycap and
LaTeX): if the picture cannot be made, the Short form is shown, never an empty
box.  The cursor on such a marker brings a message in the message line that
says why, for example {c:LaTeX marker drawn as Short: LaTeX reported: Undefined
control sequence.}, {c:LaTeX marker drawn as Short: it makes nothing visible} or
{c:LaTeX marker drawn as Short: it took more than 5 seconds}.  An error of
LaTeX is also in the log of the run ({n:appcfg-log}), with the block and the line of
the marker and the first line of what LaTeX said.  A marker that makes nothing
visible is not a failure and has no entry.  There is no dialog and nothing
waits.
Vertical space belongs in a Whitespace marker ({n:marker-whitespace}), not in a
LaTeX marker that is meant to be drawn as a picture.  A LaTeX marker that holds
vertical space and also something to see is drawn with the something to see.
.table 24 36
Text of the marker | On the screen
{c:\TeX{{}}} | The logo, a picture.
{c:{{\bfseries Bold}}} | The word in bold, a picture.
{c:$\alpha+\beta$} | The formula, a picture.
{c:\fbox{{box}}} | The word in a frame, a picture.
{c:\stratalogo[1]} | The large logo, a block of several rows.
{c:\vspace{{2ex}}} | The label: it makes nothing visible.
{c:\newcommand{{\x}}{{y}}} | The label: it makes nothing visible.
{c:\bfseries Hello} | The label: it makes nothing visible.
{c:\begin{{verbatim}}} | The label: LaTeX reports what is wrong.
{c:{{\bfseries open} | The label: LaTeX reports what is wrong.
.end
The display mode of the type LaTeX is set on the Marker Display Modes panel
({n:format-marker-modes}); with any mode but Inline (Short, Name, Long,
Diamond, Hidden) the marker is shown by that mode and no picture is made.  A
marker whose display mode is Hidden stays hidden.  The setting
{c:latex_markers} of the settings ({n:appcfg-key-latex-markers}) set to no keeps
the pictures off too: a marker that is not allowed to be printed is not drawn.
The terminal version has no pictures: a LaTeX marker whose mode is Inline is
shown in its Short form there.
{b:Documents of earlier versions.}  The setting Draw LaTeX Markers As Pictures?
of earlier versions does not exist any more.  A document that stores it is read
and the setting is ignored; it is not written again.  The default of the LaTeX
marker is Inline: a document that stores no mode for the type shows its LaTeX
markers as pictures, and the markers that give no picture in the Short form.
A document that stores a mode for the type keeps it.
.h3 When printed
The text is written into the print at the place of the marker, on lines of
their own, and LaTeX reads it as if it were part of the page.  Nothing is
added to it, nothing is escaped (a percent sign or an ampersand in it keeps
its meaning for LaTeX), and it is not wrapped or put in a box.  The text that
follows the marker goes on from where the instructions leave off, and a space
after the marker is kept.  The ends of the lines of the text are the ends of
lines of the instructions.
.h3 Examples
.table 36 25
Text of the marker | What is printed
{c:\begin{{center}}\rule{{6cm}}{{0.8pt}}\end{{center}}} | A thick rule, 6 cm long,
 | centered.
{c:\textbf{{here}}} | The word here in bold, in the line of text.
{c:\fbox{{boxed}}} | The word boxed in a frame, in the line of text.
.end
The text may have as many lines as the instructions need, such as the lines of
a table of LaTeX; each line of the marker is a line of the instructions.
.h3 The word Strata
Every print has the command {c:\strata}, like {c:\LaTeX}: it prints the word
Strata in capital letters, set as the logo of the system sets it, with the
crossbar of the first T joined to that of the second one and the letters RA
and the last A tucked under it.  The S is the drawn S of the logo, as high as
the capital letters.  It is text: it takes the size of the line
that it stands in, it sits on the baseline, and it can be used in a headline, a
note or a table.  The space after it is kept, except before a sign of
punctuation.  Put it in a LaTeX marker with the other words of the line, such
as {c:This book was set with \strata.}.
.h3 The large logo
The command {c:\stratalogo} prints the large logo: the same word, with a
large S whose top bar runs out to the right, the words Technical Document
on that bar and the word Processor over the joined crossbar.  It is made of
letters and rules, it stands on its own line, and a document uses it for a
title page or a cover.  Its normal width is about 4.4 centimeters; a number in
square brackets after the command makes it bigger or smaller, so that
{c:\stratalogo[2]} is twice as wide and {c:\stratalogo[0.5]} half as wide.  A
file made by Export as LaTeX ({n:file-export-latex}) that uses it needs no
other file.
.h3 A mistake in the text
LaTeX reports a mistake in the instructions, such as a command that does not
exist or a group that is not closed.  The mistake is an error of the document:
it is in the Error Log at the end of the print and at the block and the line of
the marker, with what LaTeX said ({n:print-errors}), and a Print Error marker
stands in front of it, which {k:Ctrl-F8} finds ({n:markers-errors}).  The
print is made as well as it can be, and a note reports that it is inaccurate.
The
Printer cannot repair instructions that are not balanced, such as an
environment that is not ended, and it does not hide them either.
.h3 What the text may hold
The text goes into the page, not into the front matter of the print, so a
command that LaTeX only allows at the beginning of a document, such as
{c:\usepackage}, gives an error.  The commands that every print has are the
ones that the text can use.
.h3 Safety
A LaTeX marker can do what its text says, and it is as powerful as the text it
holds.  A print never lets the text run a program or write a file outside the
print: a command that attempts it is rejected and is an error of the document.
Text can still read the files that LaTeX can read and fill the pages with any
content.  A document that comes from someone you do not know should
be read before it is printed.  The markers of such a document can be left out
of the print with the setting {c:latex_markers} ({n:appcfg-key-latex-markers}):
with it off, a LaTeX marker is printed as its words, and each one is a warning in the
Error Log.
.h3 Edges
- A marker without text prints nothing.
- Text of more than 65535 characters is not written: the marker is printed as
  its label and the Error Log reports this.
- The Search command and the spell check do not look into the text.
- Export as LaTeX writes the text of the marker as it is, like a print.

.h2 Line [marker-line]
.ix Line marker
.ix Marker, Line
.ix Rules and leaders
.ix Horizontal line
.ix Row of characters
A Line marker draws a horizontal line in the text: a plain rule, or a row of
one or more characters repeated, such as a row of asterisks or of dots.  Use it
for the rule under a heading, for the blank to be filled in on a form, or for
a row of dashes that separates two parts of a page.  The line begins where the
marker stands and runs to the right margin, or for the length that you give.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Length, Weight, Center, String.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Length | A number with up to three decimals, in inches; or RM | RM | The
 length of the line.  RM stands for the right margin: the line runs from the
 marker to the end of the line of text, and the text that follows the marker
 in the same paragraph is set at the right end of the line.  A length of 0
 prints as RM.  The letters must be capitals: rm in small letters is not a
 number and gives Not a valid number (Length).
Weight | A number with up to three decimals, in inches | Empty | The
 thickness of the rule.  An empty field gives the thickness of a hairline
 (0.4 point).  Used only when String is empty.
Center | A whole number | Empty | Shown in the label of the marker.  The rule
 is put a little above the base of the letters.
String | Text | Empty | The characters that make the line, repeated as often as
 they fit: *, -, = or a word.  Empty gives a rule.  The text is typed as it
 is; a character that is not on the keyboard is made as described
 in {x:edit-special-chars}.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-line.png 5.4 The panel of the Line marker: a line 3 inches long, with a weight of 0.02 inch.
Length, Weight and Center are numbers: any other text is refused with Not a
valid number and the name of the field in parentheses.  A number may not be
negative and may have no more than three decimals.  The unit is always the
inch.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Short.  The label is [LN Length:2 in] and, after it, the
fields that were set, each in the form shown: [LN Length:RM], [LN Length:2 in,
Weight:0.02 in, Center:25, String:"*-"].  A field that is not set is left out,
except the length (RM).  A number is written without trailing zeros (1.5 in, not
1.500 in).  Long mode uses the whole word, [LINE Length:2 in ...], and Name mode
[LN].  The screen does not draw the line itself.
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft (the same):
- With no String: a rule.  With a Length the rule is that long; with no Length
  (or a Length of 0) it fills the rest of the line up to the right margin.  The
  rule is as thick as the Weight, but never thinner than 0.003 inch and never
  thicker than 0.020 inch; with no Weight it is 0.4 point.
- With a String: the string is repeated as many whole times as fit in the
  length, or in the rest of the line when there is no length, and a copy that
  does not fit is left out: 1.5 inch of the string {c:*-} is eleven copies,
  22 characters, which are 1.39 inch wide.  The Weight is not used.
- The marker is part of the line of text: the words before it stay on the line
  and the words after it follow the rule (or, with no length, are set at the
  right end of the line).
.h3 Edges
- A marker alone in a block makes a block that is only a line; this is the usual
  way to rule a page.
- A Line marker is never a page break and never holds space of its own: for
  space use Whitespace ({n:marker-whitespace}).
.h3 Messages
Not a valid number (Length), Not a valid number (Weight) and Not a valid number
(Center) ({n:markers-messages}).
.h3 See
Whitespace ({n:marker-whitespace}) for vertical room; Borders and the rule under
headlines in the formats of {n:format}.

.h2 Note [marker-note]
.ix Note marker
.ix Marker, Note
.ix Footnote
.ix Levelnote
.ix Endnote
.ix Note editor
.ix NOTE EDIT
A Note marker holds the text of a note: a footnote at the foot of the page, a
levelnote at the end of a section, or an endnote on a page of its own at the end
of the document.  The marker stands in the text where the mark of the note is to
be.  The text of the note is edited in a full editor of its own, and may be as
long as you like and have several paragraphs.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | The text editor first (the status line shows NOTE EDIT),
  | then the panel with one field, Type.
Reached | The Marker Commands table, entry Note.
Global settings | Note Format and Primary Note Type of Global, Marker
  | ({n:format-marker-notes}).
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Note | The text of the note, edited in its own editor | Empty | The note.
 Press {k:Ins} in the editor to keep the text and go on to the panel, or
 {k:Esc} to cancel the whole command.  The status line of the editor reads
 Press Ins to Save Changes or Esc to Restore Previous Version.
Type | [Footnote], Footnote, Levelnote, Endnote | The Primary Note Type of the
 document, shown in brackets | The kind of the note.  The first entry shows in
 brackets the kind that the Primary Note Type of Global, Marker names
 ([Footnote], [Levelnote] or [Endnote]), so the note follows that setting if
 it is changed later; the other three entries stay in the marker.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-note-edit.png 5.4 The editor of the text of a Note marker; the status line says NOTE EDIT.
.pic pictures/markers-note-type.png 5.4 The panel of the Note marker, with the type Footnote.
{k:Space}, {k:Left} and {k:Right} step through the Type list; {k:Keypad +},
{k:Alt-+} and {k:F2} show it.  A note that is left empty stays empty; the marker
is still made.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Short: [NOTE].  Diamond: one diamond character.  Long:
[NOTE "text"] with the text of the note on one line (the ends of line become
spaces) and cut at about 95 characters.  Name: [NOTE].  The note text is not
shown in the document; put the cursor on the marker and press {k:Alt-F12} to
read
or change it.
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft:
.table 12 48
Type | What is printed
Footnote | The mark, in the style of the Reference Tag of the Footnote
  | Format (by default a small raised number), where the marker
  | stands; the note, under a short rule (the Separator Bar) at
  | the foot of the same page, in a smaller type, behind the
  | Note Tag (by default a bold number).
Levelnote | The mark where the marker stands; the note after the end of
  | the section of the level that the Note Level of the
  | Levelnote Format names (the section of the marker itself if
  | it is higher up), under a rule, in a smaller type.
Endnote | The mark where the marker stands (by default the number in
  | square brackets); all the endnotes on a page of their own
  | at the end of the document, under the Note Page Title (by
  | default Endnotes), each behind its Note Tag.
.end
Each kind is numbered by itself, in the order of the text from the beginning of
the document to its end.  The numbers, letters or marks are made from the tags
of
the three Note Format panels.  The text of a note is printed as plain text: the
attributes, the paragraphs and the tabs of the note editor are not kept, and a
line end becomes a space.
.h3 Edges
- A footnote in a headline is set after the headline has been written.  A
  footnote in the cell of a table waits until the table has ended and then goes
  to the foot of the page.
- Footnotes are always at the foot of the page and the numbers run through the
  document without starting again.
- Search does not look inside a note ({n:edit-b-search}).
.h3 See
Note Format ({n:format-marker-notes}) for the tags, the separator bar and the
page title; the Comment marker ({n:marker-comment}) for a text that is not
printed.

.h2 Orientation [marker-orientation]
.ix Orientation marker
.ix Marker, Orientation
.ix Landscape
.ix Portrait
.ix Sideways pages
An Orientation marker turns the pages that follow it from upright (portrait) to
sideways (landscape), or back.  Use it for a wide table or a picture that does
not fit across an upright page.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Orientation.
Reached | The Marker Commands table, entry Orientation.
Place | Best in a block of its own.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Orientation | [Portrait], Portrait, Landscape | [Portrait] | The way that the
 pages after the marker are set.  The first entry, in brackets, is the
 orientation that the document has (upright); it is the same as Portrait.
.end
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Short.  The label is [ORIENTATION] for portrait and
[ORIENTATION Landscape] for landscape, in Short and in Long mode; Name:
[ORIENTATION].  The screen does not turn: the text is drawn in the same way
whatever the marker says.
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft (the same):
- A Landscape marker starts a new page, and all the pages after it, up to the
  next Orientation marker, are landscape pages: the text is set along the long
  side of the paper and each of these pages is marked in the PDF as turned by 90
  degrees, so that a viewer shows it sideways.
- A Portrait marker (or [Portrait]) after landscape pages starts a new page and
  goes back to upright pages.  A Portrait marker where the pages are upright
  is ignored, not even a page break is made.
- The change is made at the beginning of the block that holds the marker, even
  if the marker is in the middle of a block; a second marker in the same block
  takes effect after the block.  Landscape pages that are still open at the
  end of the document are closed there.
- The margins, the page size and the numbering of the pages are those of the
  document.
.h3 Edges
- Two Orientation markers in a row, with text between them, make a page of
  text in the first orientation and the rest in the second.
- A Keep region that holds an Orientation marker is not kept: the Keep markers
  are dropped ({n:marker-keep}).
- Both orientations use the one list of typefaces.

.h2 Page Break [marker-page-break]
.ix Page Break marker
.ix Marker, Page Break
.ix Odd page
.ix Even page
.ix Blank pages
A Page Break marker ends the page and starts the text that follows on the next
page, or on the next odd-numbered or even-numbered page.  Use it before a
chapter that must begin on a right-hand page, or wherever the automatic breaks
do not fall where you want them.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Start Following Text on Next.
Reached | The Marker Commands table, entry Page Break.
Place | Best in a block of its own.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Start Following Text on Next | Page, Odd Page, Even Page | Page | Where the
 text after the marker begins: on the next page, on the next odd page, or on
 the next even page.
.end
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Short.  The label is [PB] for a plain break, [PB Odd Page]
and [PB Even Page] for the others, in Short and in Long mode; Name: [PB].  The
screen does not draw a line or a page: it has no page boundaries
({n:view-markers}).
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft (the same): the paragraph in which the marker stands ends there,
the page ends and the text after the marker begins at the top of the next page.
With Odd Page the next page is the next one with an odd number, and with Even
Page the next with an even number; when that is not the page that follows at once, one
page is left with nothing on it but its number.  The table shows what the
Printer made in a try with a break before each text.
.table 32 12 13
Marker (on a page of the | Text begins on | Blank page
 number) |
Page, on page 2 | 3 | None
Odd Page, on page 2 | 3 | None
Odd Page, on page 3 | 5 | Page 4
Even Page, on page 5 | 6 | None
Even Page, on page 6 | 8 | Page 7
.end
.h3 Edges
- A Page Break that is the first block after the headline of the first section
  leaves the first page with the headline only: the break always takes effect.
- In the cell of a table, and inside a Keep region, a Page Break cannot take
  effect.  In a cell it is printed as a Box with its label (PB, PB Odd Page or
  PB Even Page), and a Keep region that holds a Page Break is not kept
  ({n:marker-keep}).
- The Column Break marker ({n:marker-column-break}) makes the same page break
  and has no panel.

.h2 Page Number [marker-page-number]
.ix Page Number marker
.ix Marker, Page Number
.ix Page number in a header or footer
A Page Number marker prints the number of the page that it stands on.  It is
made for headers and footers: {c:\page\} in the Header or Footer field of a
page layout is this marker ({n:format-page-headers}).
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.
Place | A header or a footer.  The Marker Commands table of the text of a
 document shows the name in parentheses and rejects it ({n:markers-messages}).
Global setting | Page Number Tag on the Global Page Layout
 ({n:format-page-fields}).
.end
.h3 On the screen
Name, the default: [PAGE].  Short and Long show [PAGE] as well.
.h3 When printed
The number of the page, written in the style of the Page Number Tag: Arabic,
roman or letters.  The number follows the pages that the Page Number Level
numbers again ({n:format-fields-spacing}).
.h3 Edges
- The title page has no page number.
- A Page Number marker that stands in the text of a document, as a document of
  another program can have it, prints the number of the page it is on at that
  place.

.h2 Pathname [marker-pathname]
.ix Pathname marker
.ix Marker, Pathname
.ix Full path of a document
A Pathname marker prints the path of the document file: the directory and the
name.  Use it in the footer of a draft so that the paper says where its file
is.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.  The marker is inserted at once.
.end
.h3 On the screen
Long and Short, the default being Long: the path of the document in brackets,
as it was opened: /work/ann/proposal.doc.  A document that has no name
yet shows the full path under which it would be saved.  Name: [PATH].
.h3 When printed
The path as text, according to the display mode ({n:markers-print-modes}).
The path is that of your document, also when only a part of it is printed and
in Preview.  A document with no name prints PATHNAME.
.h3 Edges
- See Filename ({n:marker-filename}) for the name without the directory.

.h2 Picture [marker-picture]
.ix Picture marker
.ix Marker, Picture
.ix PNG, JPEG, PDF, EPS pictures
.ix Graphics in a document
.ix Image files
A Picture marker puts a picture from a file into the text: a drawing, a
photograph, a diagram, a scan.  The file is not copied into the document; the
document holds its name, and the Printer reads the file when the print is made.
The
files that Strata reads are PNG, JPEG, PDF and EPS files.  A picture keeps
its colors: it prints in color on a color printer and shows in color on
the screen of the X11 version.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | File Name, Position, Width, Height, Unit, Scale, Name (for
  | Table of Figures).
Reached | The Marker Commands table, entry Picture.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
File Name | A file name or path | None (required) | The picture file.
 {k:Keypad +}, {k:Alt-+} or {k:F2} show a checklist of the files that end in
 {c:.png}, {c:.jpg}, .jpeg, {c:.pdf} and .eps ({n:file-checklist}).  An empty name gives
 No string specified; the name of a file that is not there is accepted.
Position | Fixed, Floating, In Text | Fixed | On the screen In Text makes the
 picture a word that stands in a line with other text; the other two put a
 picture that is alone on its line in rows of its own.
Width | A number with up to three decimals | Empty | The width of the box that
 the picture is fitted into, in the Unit.
Height | A number with up to three decimals | Empty | The height of the box.
 With only one of Width and Height the other follows the shape of the picture;
 with both the picture is made as large as it can be inside the box and keeps
 its shape.
Unit | in, cm, mm, pt, bp, pc, dd, cc, sp, em, ex | in | The unit of Width and
 Height ({n:markers-panels}).  On paper Width and Height are always read as
 inches.
Scale | A number with up to three decimals | Empty | A factor of the picture's
 own size, for example 0.5.  Used only when both Width and Height are empty.
Name (for Table of Figures) | Text | Empty | A name for the picture, kept in the
 document.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-picture.png 5.4 The panel of the Picture marker, with a file name and a width of 2 inches.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Inline.  In the X11 version the picture is drawn in the
text, with the Width, the Height, the Scale and the Unit; until it is ready,
and when the picture cannot be made (a file that TeX cannot read, no LaTeX, or
more than five seconds), the label is shown, with the cause in the message line
and one entry in the log of the run ({n:appcfg-log}).  A picture alone on
its line takes rows of its own; with In Text it is a word of the line.  In the
terminal version, and in the other modes, the label is [PIC name] in Short and
Long and [PIC] in Name; a diamond in Diamond mode.
.h3 A picture whose file cannot be used [marker-picture-missing]
.ix Picture marker, file not found
.ix File not found, Picture marker
.ix Missing picture file
When the file of a Picture marker is not there, cannot be read or is not a
picture, the Editor does not draw the marker like the label of a picture that
is set to show in the Short or Long mode: the marker is drawn dim, with the
name and the reason, for example
{c:[PIC old-chart.png: file not found]}, in every display mode but Hidden.  In
the Long mode the label is {c:[PICTURE "old-chart.png": file not found]}.  The
reason is one of {c:file not found}, {c:cannot be read} and
{c:not a picture}.  When the cursor is on the marker the message line shows
"Picture file not found: NAME" (or "Picture file cannot be read: NAME",
"Picture file is not a picture: NAME").
.pic pictures/markers-picture-missing.png 5.5 A Picture marker whose file is not there (dim, on the first line) and a picture that is.
To mend it, put the file where the document is or correct the File Name in the
panel ({n:marker-picture}).  The Editor looks at a file that was not usable
again two seconds later, after the next key, so a file that you make or copy
while the document is open makes the marker a good one again; the cursor
coming to the marker looks at once.  The marker keeps its name, the document
is not changed, and it opens, saves and prints as any other.
.h3 Where the file is looked for [marker-picture-path]
.ix Picture marker, where the file is looked for
.ix Working directory, pictures
The Editor and the Printer use one rule for a File Name that is not a full
path (one that does not start with a slash):
- first the directory of the document;
- then the working directory (the one the panels show);
- then the directory the program was started in.
The first place that has the file wins, so a picture that is in two places is
the same one on the screen and on paper.  A full path is used as it is.  A
name that has no directory also matches any case of its letters, and back
slashes are read as slashes.
.h3 When printed
.table 10 50
Print | What the Printer does
Final | The picture is set in the line where the marker stands, as
  | large as the box shows, never wider than the line and never
  | higher than 85 percent of the text height.  With neither
  | Width, Height nor Scale it has its own size.  Width and
  | Height are read as inches.
Draft | The picture, as in Final.
Preview | As Final.
.end
.h3 Edges
- The ending of the file name does not matter: the kind of the picture is
  taken from the file.  A file that is not there, one that cannot be read and
  one that is not a PNG, JPEG, PDF or EPS picture are printed as a red box
  with the words "Picture file not found: NAME" (or the other reason), they
  are in the Error Log with the places that were looked in, and the Printer
  reports how many files were not found; the print goes on.  A file that TeX
  cannot read is printed as a box with the label, PIC name.
- A name with a directory is tried as it stands (a full path, or a path from
  the directory of the document, the working directory or the directory of
  the program).  A plain name is looked for in the directory of the document,
  then in the working directory, then in the directory of the program, and
  the letters may be of any case: Figure1.PNG finds {c:figure1.png}.  Back
  slashes are read as slashes ({n:marker-picture-path}).
- A Width of 2 with the Unit cm is 2 cm on the screen and 2 inches on paper.
- A picture can be put in a line of text with other words; the line is then as
  high as the picture.
.h3 Messages
No string specified, Not a valid number (Width), Not a valid number (Height),
Not a valid number (Scale) ({n:markers-messages}).  A picture that cannot be
made on the screen shows one of: The picture has no file name; LaTeX cannot
read that kind of picture file (use PNG, JPEG, PDF or EPS); The picture file
cannot be read; LaTeX could not make the picture (is the file a valid image?).
.h3 See
Figure and Table ({n:marker-figure}) and Equation ({n:marker-equation}); Print
and Preview in {n:print}.

.h2 Printer [marker-printer]
.ix Printer marker
.ix Marker, Printer
.ix Name of the printer of a document
A Printer marker prints the name of the printer that the document was made for.
It is meant for the page of a technical report that says on what the
document was set.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.  The marker is inserted at once.
Source of the text | The printer name of the document: Generic.
.end
.h3 On the screen
Long and Short, the default being Long: the name in brackets, such as [Generic].
Name: [PRINTER].
.h3 When printed
The name as text, by the display mode ({n:markers-print-modes}).  It is not the
queue to which the document is sent: that is chosen on the Print panel
({n:print-menu}).
.h3 Edges
The Typeface panel of Global shows the same name in its last line, Printer.
